# p_4f8b — Couple, one works from home and one commutes; the shelf is the desk's substitute

A couple lives here. Partner A works from home (09:00–17:00) and never leaves the house on weekdays. Partner B commutes to an office (08:30–17:30) and takes their own bag (object_35) and a separate set of keys or a keycard. The household keys (object_2) remain at the kitchen shelf (receptacle_11) at all times because Partner A is home and needs them for the occasional delivery or errand. Partner B enters via a separate fob or the building's keypad, so the physical keys never leave with them.

The walkthrough at 15:08 on a Monday caught Partner A at home (in the middle of a work call) and Partner B still at the office. object_14 is a reference binder or manual that lives on a shelf (receptacle_12) next to the desk; Partner A consults it in place rather than hauling it to the desk surface (receptacle_21, which holds object_20 and other active work items). The kitchen items (object_4, object_5, object_15, object_16) rotate between the counter (receptacle_4) during meal prep and their shelves (receptacle_18, receptacle_19) otherwise.

What sets this apart from p_e8f1: the keys are IN the house during weekday 9–17 h (Partner A is home), and object_14 is at receptacle_12, not at the desk or out of the house. What sets this apart from p_c9d4: two people live here, and object_35 (Partner B's bag) IS out of the house on weekdays 9–17 h. What would refute it: object_2 found out of the house on a weekday morning, or object_14 found at receptacle_21, or object_35 found in the house during weekday 9–17 h.

